1	# 3. The Newtype and Type-State Patterns 🟡
2	
3	> **What you'll learn:**
4	> - The newtype pattern for zero-cost compile-time type safety
5	> - Type-state pattern: making illegal state transitions unrepresentable
6	> - Builder pattern with type states for compile-time–enforced construction
7	> - Config trait pattern for taming generic parameter explosion
8	
9	## Newtype: Zero-Cost Type Safety
10	
11	The newtype pattern wraps an existing type in a single-field tuple struct to create a distinct type with zero runtime overhead:
12	
13	```rust
14	// Without newtypes — easy to mix up:
15	fn create_user(name: String, email: String, age: u32, employee_id: u32) { }
16	// create_user(name, email, age, id);  — but what if we swap age and id?
17	// create_user(name, email, id, age);  — COMPILES FINE, BUG
18	
19	// With newtypes — the compiler catches mistakes:
20	struct UserName(String);
21	struct Email(String);
22	struct Age(u32);
23	struct EmployeeId(u32);
24	
25	fn create_user(name: UserName, email: Email, age: Age, id: EmployeeId) { }
26	// create_user(name, email, EmployeeId(42), Age(30));
27	// ❌ Compile error: expected Age, got EmployeeId
28	```
29	
30	### `impl Deref` for Newtypes — Power and Pitfalls
31	
32	Implementing `Deref` on a newtype lets it auto-coerce to the inner type's
33	reference, giving you all of the inner type's methods "for free":
34	
35	```rust
36	use std::ops::Deref;
37	
38	struct Email(String);
39	
40	impl Email {
41	    fn new(raw: &str) -> Result<Self, &'static str> {
42	        if raw.contains('@') {
43	            Ok(Email(raw.to_string()))
44	        } else {
45	            Err("invalid email: missing @")
46	        }
47	    }
48	}
49	
50	impl Deref for Email {
51	    type Target = str;
52	    fn deref(&self) -> &str { &self.0 }
53	}
54	
55	// Now Email auto-derefs to &str:
56	let email = Email::new("user@example.com").unwrap();
57	println!("Length: {}", email.len()); // Uses str::len via Deref
58	```
59	
60	This is convenient — but it effectively **punches a hole** through your
61	newtype's abstraction boundary because *every* method on the target type
62	becomes callable on your wrapper.
63	
64	#### When `Deref` IS appropriate
65	
66	| Scenario | Example | Why it's fine |
67	|----------|---------|---------------|
68	| Smart-pointer wrappers | `Box<T>`, `Arc<T>`, `MutexGuard<T>` | The wrapper's whole purpose is to behave like `T` |
69	| Transparent "thin" wrappers | `String` → `str`, `PathBuf` → `Path`, `Vec<T>` → `[T]` | The wrapper IS-A superset of the target |
70	| Your newtype genuinely IS the inner type | `struct Hostname(String)` where you always want full string ops | Restricting the API would add no value |
71	
72	#### When `Deref` is an anti-pattern
73	
74	| Scenario | Problem |
75	|----------|---------|
76	| **Domain types with invariants** | `Email` derefs to `&str`, so callers can call `.split_at()`, `.trim()`, etc. — none of which preserve the "must contain @" invariant. If someone stores the trimmed `&str` and reconstructs, the invariant is lost. |
77	| **Types where you want a restricted API** | `struct Password(String)` with `Deref<Target = str>` leaks `.as_bytes()`, `.chars()`, `Debug` output — exactly what you're trying to hide. |
78	| **Fake inheritance** | Using `Deref` to make `ManagerWidget` auto-deref to `Widget` simulates OOP inheritance. This is explicitly discouraged — see the Rust API Guidelines (C-DEREF). |
79	
80	> **Rule of thumb**: If your newtype exists to *add type safety* or *restrict
81	> the API*, don't implement `Deref`. If it exists to *add capabilities* while
82	> keeping the inner type's full surface (like a smart pointer), `Deref` is
83	> the right choice.
84	
85	#### `DerefMut` — doubles the risk
86	
87	If you also implement `DerefMut`, callers can *mutate* the inner value
88	directly, bypassing any validation in your constructors:
89	
90	```rust
91	use std::ops::{Deref, DerefMut};
92	
93	struct PortNumber(u16);
94	
95	impl Deref for PortNumber {
96	    type Target = u16;
97	    fn deref(&self) -> &u16 { &self.0 }
98	}
99	
100	impl DerefMut for PortNumber {
101	    fn deref_mut(&mut self) -> &mut u16 { &mut self.0 }
102	}
103	
104	let mut port = PortNumber(443);
105	*port = 0; // Bypasses any validation — now an invalid port
106	```
107	
108	Only implement `DerefMut` when the inner type has no invariants to protect.
109	
110	#### Prefer explicit delegation instead
111	
112	When you want only *some* of the inner type's methods, delegate explicitly:
113	
114	```rust
115	struct Email(String);
116	
117	impl Email {
118	    fn new(raw: &str) -> Result<Self, &'static str> {
119	        if raw.contains('@') { Ok(Email(raw.to_string())) }
120	        else { Err("missing @") }
121	    }
122	
123	    // Expose only what makes sense:
124	    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str { &self.0 }
125	    pub fn len(&self) -> usize { self.0.len() }
126	    pub fn domain(&self) -> &str {
127	        self.0.split('@').nth(1).unwrap_or("")
128	    }
129	    // .split_at(), .trim(), .replace() — NOT exposed
130	}
131	```
132	
133	#### Clippy and the ecosystem
134	
135	- **`clippy::wrong_self_convention`** can fire when `Deref` coercion
136	  makes method resolution surprising (e.g., `is_empty()` resolving to the
137	  inner type's version instead of one you intended to shadow).
138	- The **Rust API Guidelines** (C-DEREF) state: *"only smart pointers
139	  should implement `Deref`."* Treat this as a strong default; deviate
140	  only with clear justification.
141	- If you need trait compatibility (e.g., passing `Email` to functions
142	  expecting `&str`), consider implementing `AsRef<str>` and `Borrow<str>`
143	  instead — they're explicit conversions without auto-coercion surprises.
144	
145	#### Decision matrix
146	
147	```text
148	Do you want ALL methods of the inner type to be callable?
149	  ├─ YES → Does your type enforce invariants or restrict the API?
150	  │    ├─ NO  → impl Deref ✅  (smart-pointer / transparent wrapper)
151	  │    └─ YES → Don't impl Deref ❌ (invariant leaks)
152	  └─ NO  → Don't impl Deref ❌  (use AsRef / explicit delegation)
153	```
154	
155	### Type-State: Compile-Time Protocol Enforcement
156	
157	The type-state pattern uses the type system to enforce that operations happen in the correct order. Invalid states become **unrepresentable**.
158	
159	```mermaid
160	stateDiagram-v2
161	    [*] --> Disconnected: new()
162	    Disconnected --> Connected: connect()
163	    Connected --> Authenticated: authenticate()
164	    Authenticated --> Authenticated: request()
165	    Authenticated --> [*]: drop
166	
167	    Disconnected --> Disconnected: ❌ request() won't compile
168	    Connected --> Connected: ❌ request() won't compile
169	```
170	
171	> Each transition *consumes* `self` and returns a new type — the compiler enforces valid ordering.
172	
173	```rust
174	// Problem: A network connection that must be:
175	// 1. Created
176	// 2. Connected
177	// 3. Authenticated
178	// 4. Then used for requests
179	// Calling request() before authenticate() should be a COMPILE error.
180	
181	// --- Type-state markers (zero-sized types) ---
182	struct Disconnected;
183	struct Connected;
184	struct Authenticated;
185	
186	// --- Connection parameterized by state ---
187	struct Connection<State> {
188	    address: String,
189	    _state: std::marker::PhantomData<State>,
190	}
191	
192	// Only Disconnected connections can connect:
193	impl Connection<Disconnected> {
194	    fn new(address: &str) -> Self {
195	        Connection {
196	            address: address.to_string(),
197	            _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
198	        }
199	    }
200	
201	    fn connect(self) -> Connection<Connected> {
202	        println!("Connecting to {}...", self.address);
203	        Connection {
204	            address: self.address,
205	            _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
206	        }
207	    }
208	}
209	
210	// Only Connected connections can authenticate:
211	impl Connection<Connected> {
212	    fn authenticate(self, _token: &str) -> Connection<Authenticated> {
213	        println!("Authenticating...");
214	        Connection {
215	            address: self.address,
216	            _state: std::marker::PhantomData,
217	        }
218	    }
219	}
220	
221	// Only Authenticated connections can make requests:
222	impl Connection<Authenticated> {
223	    fn request(&self, path: &str) -> String {
224	        format!("GET {} from {}", path, self.address)
225	    }
226	}
227	
228	fn main() {
229	    let conn = Connection::new("api.example.com");
230	    // conn.request("/data"); // ❌ Compile error: no method `request` on Connection<Disconnected>
231	
232	    let conn = conn.connect();
233	    // conn.request("/data"); // ❌ Compile error: no method `request` on Connection<Connected>
234	
235	    let conn = conn.authenticate("secret-token");
236	    let response = conn.request("/data"); // ✅ Only works after authentication
237	    println!("{response}");
238	}
239	```
240	
241	> **Key insight**: Each state transition *consumes* `self` and returns a new type.
242	> You can't use the old state after transitioning — the compiler enforces it.
243	> Zero runtime cost — `PhantomData` is zero-sized, states are erased at compile time.
244	
245	**Comparison with C++/C#**: In C++ or C#, you'd enforce this with runtime checks (`if (!authenticated) throw ...`). The Rust type-state pattern moves these checks to compile time — invalid states are literally unrepresentable in the type system.
246	
247	### Builder Pattern with Type States
248	
249	A practical application — a builder that enforces required fields:
250	
251	```rust
252	use std::marker::PhantomData;
253	
254	// Marker types for required fields
255	struct NeedsName;
256	struct NeedsPort;
257	struct Ready;
258	
259	struct ServerConfig<State> {
260	    name: Option<String>,
261	    port: Option<u16>,
262	    max_connections: usize, // Optional, has default
263	    _state: PhantomData<State>,
264	}
265	
266	impl ServerConfig<NeedsName> {
267	    fn new() -> Self {
268	        ServerConfig {
269	            name: None,
270	            port: None,
271	            max_connections: 100,
272	            _state: PhantomData,
273	        }
274	    }
275	
276	    fn name(self, name: &str) -> ServerConfig<NeedsPort> {
277	        ServerConfig {
278	            name: Some(name.to_string()),
279	            port: self.port,
280	            max_connections: self.max_connections,
281	            _state: PhantomData,
282	        }
283	    }
284	}
285	
286	impl ServerConfig<NeedsPort> {
287	    fn port(self, port: u16) -> ServerConfig<Ready> {
288	        ServerConfig {
289	            name: self.name,
290	            port: Some(port),
291	            max_connections: self.max_connections,
292	            _state: PhantomData,
293	        }
294	    }
295	}
296	
297	impl ServerConfig<Ready> {
298	    fn max_connections(mut self, n: usize) -> Self {
299	        self.max_connections = n;
300	        self
301	    }
302	
303	    fn build(self) -> Server {
304	        Server {
305	            name: self.name.unwrap(),
306	            port: self.port.unwrap(),
307	            max_connections: self.max_connections,
308	        }
309	    }
310	}
311	
312	struct Server {
313	    name: String,
314	    port: u16,
315	    max_connections: usize,
316	}
317	
318	fn main() {
319	    // Must provide name, then port, then can build:
320	    let server = ServerConfig::new()
321	        .name("my-server")
322	        .port(8080)
323	        .max_connections(500)
324	        .build();
325	
326	    // ServerConfig::new().port(8080); // ❌ Compile error: no method `port` on NeedsName
327	    // ServerConfig::new().name("x").build(); // ❌ Compile error: no method `build` on NeedsPort
328	}
329	```
