333	## Case Study: Type-Safe Connection Pool
334	
335	Real-world systems need connection pools where connections move through well-defined states. Here's how the typestate pattern enforces correctness in a production pool:
336	
337	```mermaid
338	stateDiagram-v2
339	    [*] --> Idle: pool.acquire()
340	    Idle --> Active: conn.begin_transaction()
341	    Active --> Active: conn.execute(query)
342	    Active --> Idle: conn.commit() / conn.rollback()
343	    Idle --> [*]: pool.release(conn)
344	
345	    Active --> [*]: ❌ cannot release mid-transaction
346	```
347	
348	```rust
349	use std::marker::PhantomData;
350	
351	// States
352	struct Idle;
353	struct InTransaction;
354	
355	struct PooledConnection<State> {
356	    id: u32,
357	    _state: PhantomData<State>,
358	}
359	
360	struct Pool {
361	    next_id: u32,
362	}
363	
364	impl Pool {
365	    fn new() -> Self { Pool { next_id: 0 } }
366	
367	    fn acquire(&mut self) -> PooledConnection<Idle> {
368	        self.next_id += 1;
369	        println!("[pool] Acquired connection #{}", self.next_id);
370	        PooledConnection { id: self.next_id, _state: PhantomData }
371	    }
372	
373	    // Only idle connections can be released — prevents mid-transaction leaks
374	    fn release(&self, conn: PooledConnection<Idle>) {
375	        println!("[pool] Released connection #{}", conn.id);
376	    }
377	}
378	
379	impl PooledConnection<Idle> {
380	    fn begin_transaction(self) -> PooledConnection<InTransaction> {
381	        println!("[conn #{}] BEGIN", self.id);
382	        PooledConnection { id: self.id, _state: PhantomData }
383	    }
384	}
385	
386	impl PooledConnection<InTransaction> {
387	    fn execute(&self, query: &str) {
388	        println!("[conn #{}] EXEC: {}", self.id, query);
389	    }
390	
391	    fn commit(self) -> PooledConnection<Idle> {
392	        println!("[conn #{}] COMMIT", self.id);
393	        PooledConnection { id: self.id, _state: PhantomData }
394	    }
395	
396	    fn rollback(self) -> PooledConnection<Idle> {
397	        println!("[conn #{}] ROLLBACK", self.id);
398	        PooledConnection { id: self.id, _state: PhantomData }
399	    }
400	}
401	
402	fn main() {
403	    let mut pool = Pool::new();
404	
405	    let conn = pool.acquire();
406	    let conn = conn.begin_transaction();
407	    conn.execute("INSERT INTO users VALUES ('Alice')");
408	    conn.execute("INSERT INTO orders VALUES (1, 42)");
409	    let conn = conn.commit(); // Back to Idle
410	    pool.release(conn);       // ✅ Only works on Idle connections
411	
412	    // pool.release(conn_active); // ❌ Compile error: can't release InTransaction
413	}
414	```
415	
416	**Why this matters in production**: A connection leaked mid-transaction holds database
417	locks indefinitely. The typestate pattern makes this impossible — you literally cannot
418	return a connection to the pool until the transaction is committed or rolled back.
419	
