287	## Combining Type-State with Capability Tokens
288	
289	Type-state and capability tokens compose naturally. A diagnostic that requires
290	an active IPMI session AND admin privileges:
291	
292	```rust,ignore
293	# use std::marker::PhantomData;
294	# pub struct Active;
295	# pub struct AdminToken { _p: () }
296	# pub struct IpmiSession<S> { _s: PhantomData<S> }
297	# impl IpmiSession<Active> {
298	#     pub fn send_command(&mut self, _nf: u8, _cmd: u8, _d: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8> { vec![] }
299	# }
300	
301	/// Run a firmware update — requires:
302	/// 1. Active IPMI session (type-state)
303	/// 2. Admin privileges (capability token)
304	pub fn firmware_update(
305	    session: &mut IpmiSession<Active>,   // proves session is active
306	    _admin: &AdminToken,                 // proves caller is admin
307	    image: &[u8],
308	) -> Result<(), String> {
309	    // No runtime checks needed — the signature IS the check
310	    session.send_command(0x2C, 0x01, image);
311	    Ok(())
312	}
313	```
314	
315	The caller must:
316	1. Create a session (`Idle`)
317	2. Authenticate it (`Authenticated`)
318	3. Activate it (`Active`)
319	4. Obtain an `AdminToken`
320	5. Then and only then call `firmware_update()`
321	
322	All enforced at compile time, zero runtime cost.
