324	## Beat 3: Firmware Update — Multi-Phase FSM with Composition
325	
326	A firmware update lifecycle has more states than a session and composition with
327	both capability tokens AND single-use types (ch03). This is the most complex
328	type-state example in the book — if you're comfortable with it, you've mastered
329	the pattern.
330	
331	```mermaid
332	stateDiagram-v2
333	    [*] --> Idle
334	    Idle --> Uploading : begin_upload(admin, image)
335	    Uploading --> Verifying : finish_upload()
336	    Uploading --> Idle : abort()
337	    Verifying --> Verified : verify_ok()
338	    Verifying --> Idle : verify_fail()
339	    Verified --> Applying : apply(single-use VerifiedImage token)
340	    Applying --> WaitingReboot : apply_complete()
341	    WaitingReboot --> [*] : reboot()
342	
343	    note right of Verified : VerifiedImage token consumed by apply()
344	    note right of Uploading : abort() returns to Idle (safe)
345	```
346	
347	```rust,ignore
348	use std::marker::PhantomData;
349	
350	// ── States ──
351	pub struct Idle;
352	pub struct Uploading;
353	pub struct Verifying;
354	pub struct Verified;
355	pub struct Applying;
356	pub struct WaitingReboot;
357	
358	// ── Single-use proof that image passed verification (ch03) ──
359	pub struct VerifiedImage {
360	    _private: (),
361	    pub digest: [u8; 32],
362	}
363	
364	// ── Capability token: only admins can initiate (ch04) ──
365	pub struct FirmwareAdminToken { _private: () }
366	
367	pub struct FwUpdate<S> {
368	    version: String,
369	    _state: PhantomData<S>,
370	}
371	
372	impl FwUpdate<Idle> {
373	    pub fn new() -> Self {
374	        FwUpdate { version: String::new(), _state: PhantomData }
375	    }
376	
377	    /// Begin upload — requires admin privilege.
378	    pub fn begin_upload(
379	        self,
380	        _admin: &FirmwareAdminToken,
381	        version: &str,
382	    ) -> FwUpdate<Uploading> {
383	        println!("Uploading firmware v{version}...");
384	        FwUpdate { version: version.to_string(), _state: PhantomData }
385	    }
386	}
387	
388	impl FwUpdate<Uploading> {
389	    pub fn finish_upload(self) -> FwUpdate<Verifying> {
390	        println!("Upload complete, verifying v{}...", self.version);
391	        FwUpdate { version: self.version, _state: PhantomData }
392	    }
393	
394	    /// Abort returns to Idle — safe at any point during upload.
395	    pub fn abort(self) -> FwUpdate<Idle> {
396	        println!("Upload aborted.");
397	        FwUpdate { version: String::new(), _state: PhantomData }
398	    }
399	}
400	
401	impl FwUpdate<Verifying> {
402	    /// On success, produces a single-use VerifiedImage token.
403	    pub fn verify_ok(self, digest: [u8; 32]) -> (FwUpdate<Verified>, VerifiedImage) {
404	        println!("Verification passed for v{}", self.version);
405	        (
406	            FwUpdate { version: self.version, _state: PhantomData },
407	            VerifiedImage { _private: (), digest },
408	        )
409	    }
410	
411	    pub fn verify_fail(self) -> FwUpdate<Idle> {
412	        println!("Verification failed — returning to idle.");
413	        FwUpdate { version: String::new(), _state: PhantomData }
414	    }
415	}
416	
417	impl FwUpdate<Verified> {
418	    /// Apply CONSUMES the VerifiedImage token — can't apply twice.
419	    pub fn apply(self, proof: VerifiedImage) -> FwUpdate<Applying> {
420	        println!("Applying v{} (digest: {:02x?})", self.version, &proof.digest[..4]);
421	        // proof is moved — can't be reused
422	        FwUpdate { version: self.version, _state: PhantomData }
423	    }
424	}
425	
426	impl FwUpdate<Applying> {
427	    pub fn apply_complete(self) -> FwUpdate<WaitingReboot> {
428	        println!("Apply complete — waiting for reboot.");
429	        FwUpdate { version: self.version, _state: PhantomData }
430	    }
431	}
432	
433	impl FwUpdate<WaitingReboot> {
434	    pub fn reboot(self) {
435	        println!("Rebooting into v{}...", self.version);
436	    }
437	}
438	
439	// ── Usage ──
440	
441	fn firmware_workflow() {
442	    let fw = FwUpdate::new();
443	
444	    // fw.finish_upload();  // ❌ no method `finish_upload` on FwUpdate<Idle>
445	
446	    let admin = FirmwareAdminToken { _private: () }; // from auth system
447	    let fw = fw.begin_upload(&admin, "2.10.1");
448	    let fw = fw.finish_upload();
449	
450	    let digest = [0xAB; 32]; // computed during verification
451	    let (fw, token) = fw.verify_ok(digest);
452	
453	    let fw = fw.apply(token);
454	    // fw.apply(token);  // ❌ use of moved value: `token`
455	
456	    let fw = fw.apply_complete();
457	    fw.reboot();
458	}
459	```
460	
461	**What the three beats illustrate together:**
462	
463	| Beat | Protocol | States | Composition |
464	|:----:|----------|:------:|-------------|
465	| 1 | IPMI session | 4 | Pure type-state |
466	| 2 | PCIe LTSSM | 5 | Type-state + recovery branch |
467	| 3 | Firmware update | 6 | Type-state + capability tokens (ch04) + single-use proof (ch03) |
468	
469	Each beat adds a layer of complexity. By beat 3, the compiler enforces state
470	ordering, admin privilege, AND one-time application — three bug classes
471	eliminated in a single FSM.
472	
473	### When to Use Type-State
474	
475	| Protocol | Type-State worthwhile? |
476	|----------|:------:|
477	| IPMI session lifecycle | ✅ Yes — authenticate → activate → command → close |
478	| PCIe link training | ✅ Yes — detect → poll → configure → L0 |
479	| TLS handshake | ✅ Yes — ClientHello → ServerHello → Finished |
480	| USB enumeration | ✅ Yes — Attached → Powered → Default → Addressed → Configured |
481	| Simple request/response | ⚠️ Probably not — only 2 states |
482	| Fire-and-forget messages | ❌ No — no state to track |
483	
484	## Exercise: USB Device Enumeration Type-State
485	
486	Model a USB device that must go through: `Attached` → `Powered` → `Default` → `Addressed` → `Configured`. Each transition should consume the previous state and produce the next. `send_data()` should only be available in `Configured`.
487	
